    description: "Zenodo archive of GREEN ALIBI v1.1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21762501"
abstract: >-
  A satellite remote-sensing study testing whether Solar-Induced Fluorescence
  (SIF) signals agricultural drought earlier than NDVI in the eight districts
  of Marathwada, Maharashtra, over nine Kharif seasons (2015-2023), comparing
  cropland-weighted GOSIF v2 SIF with MODIS NDVI timed to each pixel's
  observation date. Standardised against normal years, SIF did not flag
  drought before NDVI: both crossed z < -3 on the same date in 2015 and NDVI
  was eight days earlier in 2018; across 15 drought district-years the two
  coincided in 6, NDVI was earlier in 5 and SIF in 1. Scaled over the whole
  season, SIF's normal decline came before NDVI's in all nine years (8.0-34.2
  days), but NDVI does not return to its pre-season level after the monsoon;
  scaled from peak to post-peak minimum the lead falls to -5.5 to +13.4 days,
  is absent in three years, and district differences disappear (Friedman
  p = 0.97). TROPOMI SIF (2018-2020) put SIF first under both scalings;
  MODIS NIRv and EVI lead NDVI as much as SIF does and SIF does not lead
  NIRv, so the remaining lead is a property of NDVI. Only
  2015 and 2018 met the CHIRPS rainfall drought criterion; applied unchanged
  to 2001-2014 (five more drought years) as an out-of-sample test, the methods
  gave the same district-level answer (NDVI earlier in 17 of 22 drought
  district-years flagged by both at z < -1.5, SIF in 1), while regionally SIF
  flagged two of five drought years at z < -3 and NDVI one. An approximation of
  the rainfall-based Trigger-1 never fired at the regional scale and fired in
  2 of 15 drought district-years, also when evaluated per pixel. In this dataset SIF is not an earlier
  drought alarm than NDVI, and its seasonal lead is largely a property of how
  NDVI's decline is measured.
